= Error compounding in sequential systems — Technical =

== State-transition formulation ==
Consider:

<pre>
s_(t+1) = F(s_t, a_t, w_t)
a_t = π(ŝ_t)
ŝ_t = s_t + e_t
</pre>

An estimation or decision error <code>e_t</code> can alter <code>a_t</code>, which changes <code>s_(t+1)</code>. The future error distribution is therefore endogenous to prior decisions.

This distinguishes sequential compounding from independent measurement error.

== Distribution shift ==
In behavior cloning, training examples are drawn from the expert-induced state distribution <code>d_π*</code>, but deployment visits states under the learned policy <code>d_π</code>.

A mistake changes visitation:

<pre>
d_π ≠ d_π*
</pre>

and the policy may be least reliable precisely in states it did not observe during training.

DAgger addresses this by iteratively collecting labels on states visited by the learner's own policy.

== Autoregressive exposure ==
For an autoregressive model:

<pre>
p(y_1:T | x) = ∏ p(y_t | y_&lt;t, x)
</pre>

teacher-forced training conditions on reference prefixes, while free-running generation conditions on sampled/model prefixes.

An early token error therefore changes the conditioning context for all later factors.

== Local stability ==
For a differentiable deterministic transition, small perturbations can be approximated by:

<pre>
δs_(t+1) ≈ J_t δs_t + B_t δa_t
</pre>

where <code>J_t</code> is a local Jacobian.

If perturbations are repeatedly expanded by the transition/controller dynamics, error can grow. If the closed-loop system is contractive or corrective, perturbations can decay.

Hence there is no universal exponential-growth law.

== Feedback ==
Negative feedback can reduce trajectory divergence by incorporating new measurements:

<pre>
prediction
→ observation
→ residual/error
→ corrected estimate/action
</pre>

Open-loop rollouts lack this correction until a later observation or replan.

== Persistent-agent amplification ==
A durable memory write introduces a longer loop:

<pre>
e_t
→ state write m
→ later retrieval of m
→ altered policy input
→ new action/error
→ possible additional write
</pre>

This can move error propagation from one trajectory to multiple future trajectories.

== Evaluation ==
Long-horizon evaluation should therefore test more than one-step accuracy.

Relevant measures can include:

* rollout loss versus horizon;
* state-distribution divergence;
* recovery after injected perturbations;
* probability of returning to a safe set;
* error after re-grounding;
* sensitivity to contaminated memory;
* dependence between successive errors.

